If a line can be drawn tangent to a circle a the point $(5,5)$, then it must be possible to draw a radius from the center of the circle to the point $(5,5)$.  This radius will have a slope of: $$\frac{5-2}{5-3}=\frac{3}{2}$$ A key fact to remember is that tangents to a circle at a certain point are perpendicular to radii drawn from the center of the circle to that point.  This diagram summarizes that fact: [asy]
